WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:03.103
- Let me also explain to
you and your audience,

00:00:03.103 --> 00:00:05.405
how the Founding Fathers

00:00:05.405 --> 00:00:07.807
as a whole understood
the issue of slavery,

00:00:07.807 --> 00:00:11.678 align:start size:97% position:3%
because it's simply not true
that all Founding Fathers

00:00:11.678 --> 00:00:14.147
were slaveholders,
that's just not true.

00:00:14.147 --> 00:00:16.883
(dramatic music)

00:00:18.284 --> 00:00:20.387 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
- I'm Dave Rubin, and
this is "The Rubin Report"

00:00:20.387 --> 00:00:23.156 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
reminder guys, to subscribe
to our YouTube channel

00:00:23.156 --> 00:00:26.026 line:0%
and click that pesky
notification bell.

00:00:26.026 --> 00:00:29.629 align:start position:0%
Joining me today, is a Professor
of Political Philosophy

00:00:29.629 --> 00:00:32.265
at Clemson University and
author of the new book,

00:00:32.265 --> 00:00:34.834
"America's Revolutionary Mind:

00:00:34.834 --> 00:00:37.837 align:start size:84% position:16%
A Moral History of the
American Revolution

00:00:37.837 --> 00:00:41.474 align:start position:0%
and the Declaration That Defined
It," C. Bradley Thompson,

00:00:41.474 --> 00:00:43.476 align:start size:84% position:16%
welcome back to
"The Rubin Report."

00:00:43.476 --> 00:00:45.779
- Hi Dave, great
to see you again.

00:00:45.779 --> 00:00:48.481
- It's good to have you Brad

00:00:48.481 --> 00:00:51.151
well, we have to do what we
legally have to do first,

00:00:51.151 --> 00:00:54.554
which is I have to ask you
how you're coping with COVID?

00:00:54.554 --> 00:00:56.423
How's it going down
at the University?

00:00:56.423 --> 00:00:58.658 align:start size:88% position:13%
How's life what's going
on with the school

00:00:58.658 --> 00:00:59.626
and the rest of that stuff?

00:00:59.626 --> 00:01:01.194
Before we get into the book.

00:01:01.194 --> 00:01:03.430 line:0%
- Sure, everything's fine here.

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:06.566 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
The university has been closed
now for about two months.

00:01:06.566 --> 00:01:10.804 line:0%
We finished the last month
of the semester or so online,

00:01:10.804 --> 00:01:12.906
which was new and different,

00:01:12.906 --> 00:01:14.340 align:start size:84% position:16%
but it actually
worked pretty well.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:17.010
I teach a seminar
with 10 students

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:19.712
and we did it by zoom
and that was fine.

00:01:19.712 --> 00:01:23.316 align:start size:94% position:6%
But otherwise I've been
setting every day, all day

00:01:23.316 --> 00:01:25.418
in my, what I call
my redneck office,

00:01:25.418 --> 00:01:29.656
which is outside here
in warm South Carolina

00:01:29.656 --> 00:01:31.958 align:start size:84% position:16%
and working on
writing my next book.

00:01:32.992 --> 00:01:34.994 align:start size:84% position:16%
- You're a capitalist,
I know that.

00:01:34.994 --> 00:01:39.432
You teach about capitalism,
you're a free market guy,

00:01:39.432 --> 00:01:42.202
how do you feel about
the state of capitalism

00:01:42.202 --> 00:01:44.938 align:start size:97% position:3%
and the free markets in
the midst of this lockdown?

00:01:46.172 --> 00:01:48.141
- Well, I don't feel
very good about it.

00:01:48.141 --> 00:01:50.777
I'm actually genuinely worried

00:01:50.777 --> 00:01:52.479
about where this
country is headed.

00:01:52.479 --> 00:01:55.281
And I don't mean just the
current economic crisis,

00:01:55.281 --> 00:02:00.220
I mean, it is ironic to me
that that China passed along

00:02:01.387 --> 00:02:03.590
this coronavirus to
the United States.

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:05.091
And in response,

00:02:05.091 --> 00:02:08.761
our government is turning more
into the Chinese government.

00:02:08.761 --> 00:02:11.064 align:start size:97% position:3%
We're becoming considerably
more authoritarian

00:02:12.065 --> 00:02:13.566
than we ever have been before.

00:02:13.566 --> 00:02:16.870
And I'm genuinely concerned
about the freedoms and rights

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:20.473
of ordinary everyday people
to go about their business,

00:02:20.473 --> 00:02:23.776
to work and to live their lives.

00:02:23.776 --> 00:02:27.213 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
- So I've had a
bunch of different
senators and economists

00:02:27.213 --> 00:02:30.450 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
talking about, what to do with
stimulus and all this stuff

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:33.653 align:start position:0% line:0%
from Marco Rubio and Rand Paul,
and a couple other people,

00:02:33.653 --> 00:02:36.689
what would be the sort of
pure capitalist approach?

00:02:36.689 --> 00:02:39.359
We've got, something
creeping up to now,

00:02:39.359 --> 00:02:41.294
40 million unemployed people,

00:02:41.294 --> 00:02:43.863
we're not letting enough
people go back to work

00:02:43.863 --> 00:02:45.431
some of the States
are doing things

00:02:45.431 --> 00:02:46.900
a little bit differently,

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:48.401 align:start size:94% position:6%
but as you know, I'm
here in California where,

00:02:48.401 --> 00:02:50.937 align:start position:0%
we're still in lockdown now
they're telling us till August,

00:02:50.937 --> 00:02:52.805 align:start size:84% position:16%
even though we
flattened the curve,

00:02:52.805 --> 00:02:54.507
we did what they asked

00:02:54.507 --> 00:02:57.744
yet they seem to still roll
out these draconian measures.

00:02:57.744 --> 00:03:01.481
What's the right policy if
you believe in free markets,

00:03:01.481 --> 00:03:02.882
if you believe in capitalism

00:03:02.882 --> 00:03:05.485
and you believe in
human ingenuity?

00:03:05.485 --> 00:03:06.986
- Yeah, I think the simplest

00:03:06.986 --> 00:03:10.557
and the first and foremost
thing is you government,

00:03:10.557 --> 00:03:13.193
I think probably has
an appropriate role

00:03:13.193 --> 00:03:16.629
to protect those who
are most vulnerable,

00:03:16.629 --> 00:03:18.164
to protect the elderly.

00:03:19.465 --> 00:03:24.103 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I think veterans
facilities and old folks homes

00:03:24.971 --> 00:03:25.705
could have been on lockdown,

00:03:26.873 --> 00:03:28.374
but I don't think that
the rest of the country

00:03:28.374 --> 00:03:30.143
should have been on lockdown

00:03:30.143 --> 00:03:35.148 align:start position:0%
and we are free rational
individuals and we have rights.

00:03:36.316 --> 00:03:39.452 align:start size:88% position:13%
And each of us has to
assess the risks ourself

00:03:39.452 --> 00:03:42.021 align:start size:88% position:13%
and we have to take
the appropriate actions

00:03:42.021 --> 00:03:46.292
that we think will protect
ourselves, our families,

00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:49.329
our coworkers, and the
members of our community.

00:03:49.329 --> 00:03:52.665
And I think Americans have by
and large done exactly that.

00:03:52.665 --> 00:03:54.167
I mean, it's been remarkable,

00:03:55.435 --> 00:03:59.372 align:start size:88% position:13%
what ordinary, everyday
free people can do

00:03:59.372 --> 00:04:02.775
to protect themselves, I mean,
nobody wants the coronavirus.

00:04:02.775 --> 00:04:04.811
And I think the Americans
without the government

00:04:04.811 --> 00:04:09.816
have done an extraordinary job
in taking the necessary steps

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:12.018
to prevent getting it.

00:04:12.018 --> 00:04:13.987 align:start size:97% position:3%
- So what do you think the
role of the government then is

00:04:13.987 --> 00:04:15.622 align:start size:84% position:16%
at the state level
and the federal level?

00:04:15.622 --> 00:04:17.023
What do you think the role is

00:04:17.023 --> 00:04:19.225 align:start size:88% position:13%
when they're not letting
people go to work?

00:04:19.225 --> 00:04:21.494
Is it to bail out everybody,

00:04:21.494 --> 00:04:23.329 align:start size:88% position:13%
is it to help small
business, big business,

00:04:23.329 --> 00:04:26.666
some combination thereof,
is there a role at all?

00:04:26.666 --> 00:04:29.936
- Yeah, I don't think that
there's really very much role

00:04:29.936 --> 00:04:34.941
certainly for paying the
American people not to work

00:04:36.342 --> 00:04:37.644
and for all of the bail outs.

00:04:37.644 --> 00:04:41.414 align:start size:88% position:13%
I mean, because I would
have had a lockdown

00:04:41.414 --> 00:04:45.051 align:start size:94% position:6%
that would have been
much, much less draconian.

00:04:45.051 --> 00:04:50.056
And so therefore the economy
would've not been burdened

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:52.792
by this and ordinary,
everyday Americans

00:04:54.127 --> 00:04:56.596 align:start size:81% position:19%
would have just
continued working.

00:04:56.596 --> 00:04:58.464
- All right, so let's
shift to your book

00:04:58.464 --> 00:05:00.667 align:start position:0%
because some of the things that
you talk about in the book,

00:05:00.667 --> 00:05:02.902 align:start size:97% position:3%
actually, probably everything
you talk about the book,

00:05:02.902 --> 00:05:04.804 align:start size:88% position:13%
we can frame around
sort of what's going on

00:05:04.804 --> 00:05:05.972
with us right now.

00:05:05.972 --> 00:05:07.707 align:start size:84% position:16%
And as I was reading
it, I thought,

00:05:07.707 --> 00:05:11.177
"Man, this guy hits
every buzzword that
any meaning to me."

00:05:11.177 --> 00:05:13.513 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I'm just gonna read
the first couple sentences

00:05:13.513 --> 00:05:15.114 align:start size:88% position:13%
of the inner flap 'cause
I think that really

00:05:15.114 --> 00:05:16.349
can almost set us off

00:05:16.349 --> 00:05:17.850
for the whole rest
of the conversation.

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:19.752
'Cause you're really trying
to do two things in this book

00:05:19.752 --> 00:05:21.421
that I think you do quite well.

00:05:21.421 --> 00:05:23.956
So it says "The purpose
of this book is two-fold,

00:05:23.956 --> 00:05:26.759
"to elucidate the
logic, principles

00:05:26.759 --> 00:05:29.829 align:start size:97% position:3%
"and significance of "The
Declaration of Independence"

00:05:29.829 --> 00:05:32.799
"as the embodiment
of the American mind,

00:05:32.799 --> 00:05:35.168
"and to shed light
on what John Adams

00:05:35.168 --> 00:05:38.604
"once called "the real
American Revolution".

00:05:38.604 --> 00:05:41.174 align:start size:88% position:13%
"That is the moral
revolution that occurred

00:05:41.174 --> 00:05:46.045
"in the minds of the people
in the 15 years before 1776."

00:05:46.045 --> 00:05:49.649 align:start size:97% position:3%
So that's the part that I'm
super interested in right now,

00:05:49.649 --> 00:05:52.385 align:start size:84% position:16%
because it seems to
me in 2020 with corona

00:05:52.385 --> 00:05:56.823
and everything else that we
are in some sort of idea,

00:05:56.823 --> 00:05:58.758
churning state right now

00:05:58.758 --> 00:06:01.828 align:start position:0%
that may be could 15 years
from now, we'll go back and go,

00:06:01.828 --> 00:06:04.864
"Whoa, this is where a lot of
these ideas actually started."

00:06:04.864 --> 00:06:07.433 align:start size:84% position:16%
So let's start with
the first part though.

00:06:07.433 --> 00:06:09.235
"The Declaration
of Independence"

00:06:09.235 --> 00:06:12.672
as the embodiment of
"The American Mind."

00:06:12.672 --> 00:06:14.540
Can you talk about
that a little bit?

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:15.475
- Yeah, sure.

00:06:15.475 --> 00:06:18.010
So in 1825, just before he died,

00:06:18.010 --> 00:06:20.980
Thomas Jefferson wrote a famous
letter in which he described

00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:23.716 align:start size:97% position:3%
the object of "The
Declaration of Independence"

00:06:23.716 --> 00:06:27.887
as an expression of
"The American Mind."

00:06:27.887 --> 00:06:30.323
And that statement
has always struck me.

00:06:30.323 --> 00:06:35.094
So that indicates that "The
Declaration of Independence"

00:06:35.094 --> 00:06:36.996
is a kind of summing up.

00:06:36.996 --> 00:06:41.801 align:start size:94% position:6%
It tells us what Americans
were thinking in 1776.

00:06:41.801 --> 00:06:44.337
It tells us what they thought

00:06:44.337 --> 00:06:47.273 align:start size:94% position:6%
their moral and political
principles were.

00:06:47.273 --> 00:06:50.676 align:start size:97% position:3%
And the declaration lays
out four self-evident truths

00:06:50.676 --> 00:06:53.045 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I think those four
self-evident truths

00:06:53.045 --> 00:06:55.281
are at the heart
and soul of what

00:06:55.281 --> 00:06:57.683 align:start size:88% position:13%
the American Revolution
was all about

00:06:57.683 --> 00:07:00.653
and subsequently what the
United States of America

00:07:00.653 --> 00:07:02.221
has always been.

00:07:02.221 --> 00:07:06.592 align:start size:88% position:13%
So it is the declaration
is the heart and soul

00:07:06.592 --> 00:07:08.428
of the United States.

00:07:08.428 --> 00:07:11.497
It provides the
moral foundation.

00:07:11.497 --> 00:07:13.065
So it's on the one hand,

00:07:13.065 --> 00:07:15.968 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's a summing up of the
principles of the revolution,

00:07:15.968 --> 00:07:17.570
but it's more than that.

00:07:17.570 --> 00:07:22.575
It's also a statement of who
and what we are as a people,

00:07:23.776 --> 00:07:25.611
it's at the heart and
soul of the philosophy

00:07:25.611 --> 00:07:28.581
that I call Americanism.

00:07:28.581 --> 00:07:31.083 align:start size:88% position:13%
- So you mentioned those
self-evident truths

00:07:31.083 --> 00:07:33.853 align:start size:94% position:6%
that we hold these truths
to be self-evident.

00:07:33.853 --> 00:07:37.223
That's what it says in "The
Declaration of Independence".

00:07:37.223 --> 00:07:40.259 align:start size:88% position:13%
Why is in and of itself,
is that so important?

00:07:40.259 --> 00:07:42.895 align:start size:94% position:6%
This, we hold these
truths to be self-evident

00:07:42.895 --> 00:07:44.730
that we don't have to relitigate

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:47.166 align:start size:84% position:16%
that some things are
because they are.

00:07:48.568 --> 00:07:50.403 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, so I think the first
and most important thing

00:07:50.403 --> 00:07:55.408
to note Dave, is that "The
Revolutionary Generation"

00:07:56.576 --> 00:07:59.145
actually believed in
the concept of truth.

00:07:59.145 --> 00:08:02.915 align:start size:97% position:3%
They believed that there
are certain moral principles

00:08:02.915 --> 00:08:07.019
that are objectively,
absolutely, permanently

00:08:07.019 --> 00:08:09.589
and universally true.

00:08:09.589 --> 00:08:11.958 align:start size:88% position:13%
And that is very
different from the world

00:08:11.958 --> 00:08:13.125
in which we live today.

00:08:13.125 --> 00:08:14.660 align:start size:81% position:19%
So in 2016--
- Can you lay out

00:08:16.128 --> 00:08:17.563
some of those principles just
so we can put them in contrast

00:08:17.563 --> 00:08:19.298
to kind of where
we're at right now?

00:08:19.298 --> 00:08:22.435
- Yeah, so I mean, the
first thing to say is

00:08:22.435 --> 00:08:25.271
we live in a postmodern age.

00:08:25.271 --> 00:08:28.574
In 2016, the Oxford Dictionary

00:08:29.976 --> 00:08:33.246 align:start size:94% position:6%
described its Word of
the Year as "post-truth."

00:08:33.246 --> 00:08:36.983
And "Time Magazine" ran a
cover asking the question

00:08:36.983 --> 00:08:38.417
"Is truth dead?"

00:08:38.417 --> 00:08:40.786
So in 21st century America,

00:08:40.786 --> 00:08:45.758 align:start size:94% position:6%
we no longer believe in
the very concept, 'truth.'

00:08:45.758 --> 00:08:47.927 align:start size:84% position:16%
And that is
dramatically different

00:08:47.927 --> 00:08:50.530 align:start size:97% position:3%
from what "The Revolutionary
Generation" believed.

00:08:50.530 --> 00:08:53.533 align:start size:97% position:3%
They, as I said, they
believed in the concept truth

00:08:53.533 --> 00:08:56.536 align:start size:88% position:13%
that there are absolute,
certain, morally,

00:08:56.536 --> 00:09:00.806
universal principles and
the particular principles

00:09:00.806 --> 00:09:04.176
that they believed in the
four self-evident truths

00:09:04.176 --> 00:09:05.444
of the declaration,

00:09:05.444 --> 00:09:07.446
which are contained in
the second paragraph.

00:09:07.446 --> 00:09:09.515
I think each one of
them can be summed up

00:09:09.515 --> 00:09:14.387 align:start size:88% position:13%
in one word and they
would be first equality,

00:09:14.387 --> 00:09:19.392 align:start position:0%
second, rights, third,
consent, and fourth revolution.

00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:22.662
Now you have to read
the second paragraph

00:09:22.662 --> 00:09:27.667 align:start position:0%
to get the full flavor of
those four self-evident truths.

00:09:27.667 --> 00:09:32.171
But that is the core of
what Thomas Jefferson

00:09:32.171 --> 00:09:36.676 align:start size:94% position:6%
referred to as in my
view, "The American mind."

00:09:36.676 --> 00:09:39.245
- So let's preempt the haters

00:09:39.245 --> 00:09:40.513
who will say, "Wait a minute,

00:09:40.513 --> 00:09:43.349
"how could a quality be in there

00:09:43.349 --> 00:09:45.818
"because not everyone
was created equal,

00:09:45.818 --> 00:09:47.587 align:start size:84% position:16%
"we know men and
women were not equal,

00:09:47.587 --> 00:09:50.590 align:start size:97% position:3%
"we know these were slave
owners, et cetera, et cetera?"

00:09:50.590 --> 00:09:52.058 align:start size:88% position:13%
We've talked about this
a little bit before

00:09:52.058 --> 00:09:53.459
when you were on
the show last time,

00:09:53.459 --> 00:09:58.030
but can you unpack that
for us a little bit?

00:09:58.030 --> 00:10:01.701 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Yeah, so that's, it's
both a complicated,

00:10:01.701 --> 00:10:06.238 align:start size:84% position:16%
and obviously now a
controversial subject,

00:10:06.238 --> 00:10:08.240
particularly in the light

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:10.910 align:start size:84% position:16%
of the New York
Times's 1619 Project

00:10:10.910 --> 00:10:13.412 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I'm sure your audience
and you, of course,

00:10:13.412 --> 00:10:15.681
you're very familiar
with The 1619 Project

00:10:15.681 --> 00:10:18.584 align:start size:97% position:3%
The purpose of which is to
argue that America was founded

00:10:18.584 --> 00:10:21.988
not in 1776, but rather in 1619

00:10:21.988 --> 00:10:25.257
when the first slaves were
brought to the United States.

00:10:25.257 --> 00:10:28.928 align:start size:97% position:3%
And what that means of course,
is that the United States

00:10:28.928 --> 00:10:33.833
is founded on slavery,
which therefore means

00:10:33.833 --> 00:10:37.803
that it is by definition
immoral and evil, right?

00:10:37.803 --> 00:10:42.375 align:start size:94% position:6%
That is in sum what The
1619 project is all about.

00:10:42.375 --> 00:10:46.712 align:start size:97% position:3%
But America's Revolutionary
Founders in 1776

00:10:47.613 --> 00:10:49.915
rejected the idea of slavery.

00:10:49.915 --> 00:10:52.652
But let me unpack that because
that's obviously complicated.

00:10:52.652 --> 00:10:54.186 align:start size:97% position:3%
- [Dave] Yeah.
- So the first thing to note

00:10:54.186 --> 00:10:59.158
is that in 1776, slavery was
the norm around the world.

00:11:00.359 --> 00:11:03.362 align:start size:88% position:13%
There had always been
slavery through all time

00:11:03.362 --> 00:11:05.665
over all continents.

00:11:05.665 --> 00:11:10.670
Slavery had universally been
accepted around the world.

00:11:11.837 --> 00:11:15.708
The second thing to
note is that in 1776,

00:11:15.708 --> 00:11:20.079
no Founding Father supported
slavery as an institution.

00:11:20.079 --> 00:11:24.884 align:start size:97% position:3%
They regarded it as what
they called a necessary evil.

00:11:24.884 --> 00:11:27.753 align:start size:94% position:6%
And the emphasis has to
be put on the word "evil."

00:11:27.753 --> 00:11:29.455 align:start size:88% position:13%
Now take Thomas
Jefferson, for instance,

00:11:29.455 --> 00:11:34.460 align:start position:0%
he clearly, and explicitly
States that slavery is an evil,

00:11:35.327 --> 00:11:37.763
immoral institution, why?

00:11:37.763 --> 00:11:41.133 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
Because it violates
the doctrine of rights.

00:11:41.133 --> 00:11:44.570 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
He also believed that
slavery was an inherently,

00:11:44.570 --> 00:11:47.573 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
corrupting
institution, all right.

00:11:47.573 --> 00:11:51.644 align:start size:94% position:6%
So that's what's important
to say at the outset.

00:11:51.644 --> 00:11:55.047
Now, let me also explain
you and your audience,

00:11:55.047 --> 00:11:58.284
how the Founding
Fathers as a whole

00:11:58.284 --> 00:11:59.819
understood the issue of slavery,

00:11:59.819 --> 00:12:03.556 align:start size:97% position:3%
because it's simply not true
that all Founding Fathers

00:12:03.556 --> 00:12:05.958
were slaveholders,
that's just not true.

00:12:05.958 --> 00:12:09.695
And so you can divide the
revolutionary generation,

00:12:09.695 --> 00:12:11.797
I would say, into four camps.

00:12:11.797 --> 00:12:15.534 align:start size:97% position:3%
So first there were Founding
Fathers like John Adams,

00:12:15.534 --> 00:12:18.704 align:start size:97% position:3%
for instance, Samuel Adams,
who never owned a slave

00:12:18.704 --> 00:12:21.006
and were morally
opposed to slavery.

00:12:21.006 --> 00:12:22.975
And then there's a second camp.

00:12:24.176 --> 00:12:26.712
People like Benjamin
Franklin and John J

00:12:28.114 --> 00:12:32.585 align:start size:94% position:6%
who created the first
anti-slavery organizations

00:12:34.019 --> 00:12:35.287
in the United States, but
earlier in their lives,

00:12:35.287 --> 00:12:36.489
they had been slaveholders,

00:12:36.489 --> 00:12:38.724 align:start size:88% position:13%
but certainly they came
to condemn slavery.

00:12:38.724 --> 00:12:40.793
And then you get people
in the third category

00:12:40.793 --> 00:12:42.428
like George Washington,

00:12:42.428 --> 00:12:45.464 align:start size:94% position:6%
who yes was a slaveholder
his whole life,

00:12:45.464 --> 00:12:49.535
but upon his death freed
his slaves in his will.

00:12:49.535 --> 00:12:52.037 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then finally,
there's the hard category

00:12:52.037 --> 00:12:55.708
consisting of people
like Patrick Henry
and Thomas Jefferson

00:12:56.942 --> 00:13:01.080
who owned slaves and
who did not abolish

00:13:01.080 --> 00:13:04.583 align:start size:94% position:6%
or did not free their
slaves upon their deaths.

00:13:04.583 --> 00:13:07.853
So how are we to evaluate that?

00:13:07.853 --> 00:13:10.723 align:start size:88% position:13%
I think the first thing
to say is that both

00:13:11.857 --> 00:13:13.626 align:start size:84% position:16%
say Patrick Henry
and Thomas Jefferson,

00:13:13.626 --> 00:13:15.594 align:start size:94% position:6%
as I've already
indicated with Jefferson,

00:13:15.594 --> 00:13:18.664
were adamantly opposed to
the institution of slavery.

00:13:19.999 --> 00:13:22.301 align:start size:88% position:13%
If you read their
private correspondence,

00:13:22.301 --> 00:13:25.004
they lamented slavery.

00:13:25.004 --> 00:13:28.774
They saw themselves
as morally culpable,

00:13:28.774 --> 00:13:32.178 align:start size:84% position:16%
but they didn't
abolish their slaves

00:13:32.178 --> 00:13:33.445
and the institution of slavery,

00:13:33.445 --> 00:13:35.981
I think for two primary reasons.

00:13:37.183 --> 00:13:39.518
The first... Actually
I'd say three reasons.

00:13:39.518 --> 00:13:41.120 align:start size:84% position:16%
The first is that
they thought slavery

00:13:41.120 --> 00:13:43.622 align:start size:84% position:16%
would eventually
die a natural death.

00:13:43.622 --> 00:13:45.558
About that, they were mistaken.

00:13:45.558 --> 00:13:47.726
They could not have
imagined in 1776,

00:13:47.726 --> 00:13:49.328
the invention of the cotton gin.

00:13:50.529 --> 00:13:55.267
Secondly, both Henry
and Thomas Jefferson

00:13:58.170 --> 00:14:02.374 align:start size:84% position:16%
were deeply concerned
about what I call

00:14:02.374 --> 00:14:05.477
the post-emancipation problem.

00:14:05.477 --> 00:14:07.680 align:start size:84% position:16%
And by the post
emancipation problem,

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:10.382 align:start size:84% position:16%
what I mean is it's
the question of

00:14:10.382 --> 00:14:13.319 align:start size:84% position:16%
all right, So we free
the slaves today,

00:14:13.319 --> 00:14:16.488
which is the morally right
thing to do, but then what?

00:14:16.488 --> 00:14:19.258
And Jefferson, himself
was deeply concerned

00:14:19.258 --> 00:14:22.695
about the possibility,
the very real possibility

00:14:22.695 --> 00:14:25.497
of a race war in the Southern,

00:14:25.497 --> 00:14:28.901 align:start size:84% position:16%
particularly in the
slave majority States.

00:14:28.901 --> 00:14:32.504
So the question is how
do you free your slaves

00:14:32.504 --> 00:14:36.775 align:start size:84% position:16%
without creating
any kind of race war

00:14:38.444 --> 00:14:41.814
between whites and blacks?

00:14:41.814 --> 00:14:44.250 align:start size:84% position:16%
So, I mean, these
were the big issues.

00:14:44.250 --> 00:14:46.185 align:start size:88% position:13%
And then the last thing
I would say, Dave,

00:14:46.185 --> 00:14:50.089
is it is important and I
try to do this in my book,

00:14:50.089 --> 00:14:54.360
and that is to hold like Thomas
Jefferson, morally culpable.

00:14:54.360 --> 00:14:56.729
He was a slave owner, he
didn't free his slaves.

00:14:56.729 --> 00:14:58.864
And part of the reason
he didn't free his slaves

00:14:58.864 --> 00:15:03.802 align:start size:97% position:3%
is because of his own personal
habits, his lifestyle.

00:15:05.104 --> 00:15:07.673
Jefferson lived high

00:15:10.709 --> 00:15:11.877
and when he died,

00:15:11.877 --> 00:15:13.412
he died in debt.

00:15:13.412 --> 00:15:17.583
And as a result of living
the lifestyle that he did,

00:15:19.018 --> 00:15:22.855
he didn't have the resources
he thought to free his slaves.

00:15:22.855 --> 00:15:27.192 align:start size:97% position:3%
So we have to judge someone
like Thomas Jefferson

00:15:27.192 --> 00:15:31.797 align:start size:97% position:3%
as morally culpable, of that
there can be no question.

00:15:31.797 --> 00:15:33.098
- Yeah, so it's interesting.

00:15:33.098 --> 00:15:34.199
We talked about this last time,

00:15:34.199 --> 00:15:35.968
but, when you visit Monticello,

00:15:35.968 --> 00:15:38.337 align:start size:97% position:3%
which I've been to
probably five or six times,

00:15:38.337 --> 00:15:40.806 align:start size:84% position:16%
which was his
estate in Virginia,

00:15:40.806 --> 00:15:43.309
they spend, when you go
on the tour, they spend

00:15:43.309 --> 00:15:46.078
what I would say is almost a
disproportionate amount of time

00:15:46.078 --> 00:15:48.447
talking about slavery,
which in a weird way,

00:15:49.782 --> 00:15:51.750 align:start size:84% position:16%
even though it feels
disproportionate

00:15:51.750 --> 00:15:54.486
it's sort of like, we're
not shying away from this,

00:15:54.486 --> 00:15:56.555
I mean, they are
very open about it.

00:15:56.555 --> 00:15:58.924
And it does go to the complexity

00:15:58.924 --> 00:16:01.160 align:start size:88% position:13%
that they're not saying
he's not culpable

00:16:01.160 --> 00:16:02.628
or anything like that.

00:16:02.628 --> 00:16:04.964 align:start position:0%
Are you ever concerned that
when you write a book like this

00:16:04.964 --> 00:16:08.067 align:start size:88% position:13%
and when you talk
about these great minds

00:16:08.067 --> 00:16:11.370 align:start size:97% position:3%
that founded the country, that
so much of the conversation

00:16:11.370 --> 00:16:13.038
gets funneled to
the slavery issue,

00:16:13.038 --> 00:16:17.209
not to diminish the importance
of discussing it, obviously,

00:16:17.209 --> 00:16:19.979 align:start size:97% position:3%
but that's so much of it
seems to go in that direction

00:16:19.979 --> 00:16:23.749 align:start size:97% position:3%
because of the way we look
through a racial lens in 2020,

00:16:23.749 --> 00:16:26.485
or at least the media
does, or many of us do.

00:16:27.519 --> 00:16:28.954
- I am concerned about it

00:16:28.954 --> 00:16:33.325 align:start size:97% position:3%
because it's not what defined
the American Revolution,

00:16:33.325 --> 00:16:35.861
and it's not what defines
the American Founding.

00:16:35.861 --> 00:16:37.963 align:start size:84% position:16%
In fact, I would argue
just the opposite.

00:16:37.963 --> 00:16:41.900
So rather than actually
lamenting the fact that

00:16:41.900 --> 00:16:44.703 align:start size:94% position:6%
it always comes up, I
take it as an opportunity

00:16:44.703 --> 00:16:48.307 align:start size:97% position:3%
to tell people that ""The
Declaration of Independence""

00:16:48.307 --> 00:16:50.042
authored by Thomas Jefferson

00:16:50.042 --> 00:16:53.812
is the single greatest
document in human history

00:16:53.812 --> 00:16:57.816 align:start size:84% position:16%
that led to the
abolition of slavery.

00:16:57.816 --> 00:17:01.687
And in the decades
immediately after

00:17:01.687 --> 00:17:04.256
the publication of
the declaration,

00:17:04.256 --> 00:17:07.226
the Northern States, in fact,
every single Northern Sate

00:17:07.226 --> 00:17:12.064 align:start size:88% position:13%
between 1776 and 1803
passed manumission laws,

00:17:12.064 --> 00:17:15.034
where they put in place laws

00:17:15.034 --> 00:17:18.404 align:start size:97% position:3%
that would lead to the gradual
emancipation of slavery.

00:17:18.404 --> 00:17:22.875 align:start size:97% position:3%
And then at the federal
level, the federal government

00:17:22.875 --> 00:17:27.679
in 1787 passed the
Northwest ordinance,

00:17:27.679 --> 00:17:29.882
which prevented the
extension of slavery

00:17:29.882 --> 00:17:31.750
into the Northwest territory,

00:17:31.750 --> 00:17:36.755
which was the land West of
the Appalachian Mountains

00:17:37.656 --> 00:17:39.625
through Ohio and Indiana.

00:17:39.625 --> 00:17:42.661 align:start size:88% position:13%
And then secondly, there
was the prohibition

00:17:42.661 --> 00:17:46.331
in the constitution of
the Atlantic Slave Trade

00:17:46.331 --> 00:17:48.434
ending in 1808.

00:17:48.434 --> 00:17:53.439
So given the time, given
the reality on the ground,

00:17:55.407 --> 00:17:59.711
they did, I think as
much as was possible,

00:17:59.711 --> 00:18:01.914
given the circumstances, right?

00:18:01.914 --> 00:18:05.784 align:start size:88% position:13%
So it's a hard question
it's complicated,

00:18:05.784 --> 00:18:09.388
but in my view, in the end,

00:18:09.388 --> 00:18:14.026 align:start size:84% position:16%
the Founding Fathers
are vindicated

00:18:14.026 --> 00:18:16.795 align:start size:84% position:16%
by the principles
that they enunciated

00:18:16.795 --> 00:18:17.930
which led...

00:18:19.031 --> 00:18:22.367 align:start size:84% position:16%
And the United States
is in many ways,

00:18:22.367 --> 00:18:25.604 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, it's history
of antislavery

00:18:25.604 --> 00:18:29.775
is simply one of the great
stories of American history

00:18:29.775 --> 00:18:33.212
through the 19th century.

00:18:33.212 --> 00:18:36.849 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so, yes, we need to
tell the story of slavery,

00:18:36.849 --> 00:18:40.486
but we also need to tell
the equally important story

00:18:40.486 --> 00:18:45.491
of how and why slavery was
ended in the United States.

00:18:46.959 --> 00:18:49.461
- So I want to back up to
those self-evident truths

00:18:49.461 --> 00:18:50.696
because it's one of the topics

00:18:50.696 --> 00:18:52.865
that I'm actually most
interested in right now

00:18:52.865 --> 00:18:55.367
and I write a little
bit about in my book

00:18:55.367 --> 00:18:58.937
that there has to be
something outside of us

00:18:58.937 --> 00:19:01.039
to organize a society.

00:19:01.039 --> 00:19:03.475 align:start size:88% position:13%
Do you know how much
sort of internal arguing

00:19:03.475 --> 00:19:07.412
they had as to what
those self-evident
truths were actually

00:19:07.412 --> 00:19:10.382 align:start size:88% position:13%
and how they all arrived
at what they were?

00:19:11.383 --> 00:19:12.985
- Yeah, that's a great question.

00:19:14.153 --> 00:19:17.055 align:start size:84% position:16%
So I would say that
the principle division

00:19:17.055 --> 00:19:22.060
amongst colonial Americans
in the 1760s and in the 1770s

00:19:23.495 --> 00:19:27.366
was between American Patriots
and the so-called Loyalists

00:19:27.366 --> 00:19:31.303 align:start size:94% position:6%
or American Tories.
That's the great division.

00:19:31.303 --> 00:19:35.674 align:start position:0%
And in the end, they had really
very different conceptions

00:19:35.674 --> 00:19:37.242
of what America ought to be.

00:19:37.242 --> 00:19:39.178 align:start size:84% position:16%
And they had very
different conceptions

00:19:39.178 --> 00:19:43.682 align:start position:0%
of the underlying moral
principles of the United States.

00:19:43.682 --> 00:19:46.585 align:start size:97% position:3%
But once "The Declaration
of Independence" was passed,

00:19:46.585 --> 00:19:49.888
that disagreement became moot.

00:19:49.888 --> 00:19:52.324 align:start size:81% position:19%
And I think what's
most important

00:19:52.324 --> 00:19:55.360
and the story that I
try to tell in my book

00:19:55.360 --> 00:20:00.232
is the story of how and
why American Patriots

00:20:00.232 --> 00:20:05.237
came to search for, discover
and develop, the principles

00:20:07.105 --> 00:20:09.608 align:start size:84% position:16%
that would ultimately
be elucidated

00:20:09.608 --> 00:20:11.476
in "The Declaration
of Independence".

00:20:11.476 --> 00:20:14.813
And so going back to
your first question

00:20:14.813 --> 00:20:17.649
and the two-fold
purpose of my book.

00:20:17.649 --> 00:20:19.151
So on the one hand,

00:20:19.151 --> 00:20:22.554
yes, the book is about "The
Declaration of Independence".

00:20:22.554 --> 00:20:26.592 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's a close textual
analysis of the declaration,

00:20:26.592 --> 00:20:30.229 align:start size:88% position:13%
particularly of the four
self-evident truths.

00:20:30.229 --> 00:20:32.864 align:start size:84% position:16%
But the book actually
in the end, Dave,

00:20:32.864 --> 00:20:35.734
is about a lot more than just

00:20:35.734 --> 00:20:37.736
"The Declaration
of Independence".

00:20:37.736 --> 00:20:39.871
It's about "The American Mind,"

00:20:39.871 --> 00:20:43.375 align:start size:94% position:6%
and it's about the
deepest causes and meaning

00:20:43.375 --> 00:20:45.277
of The American Revolution.

00:20:45.277 --> 00:20:47.079
So, as I said, the declaration

00:20:47.079 --> 00:20:48.947
is a summing up of
those principles.

00:20:48.947 --> 00:20:50.182
But the real question,

00:20:50.182 --> 00:20:52.384 align:start size:84% position:16%
the really fascinating
part of the story

00:20:52.384 --> 00:20:55.921 align:start size:94% position:6%
is how the Americans
came to those principles.

00:20:55.921 --> 00:20:58.323
Because they didn't
hold them necessarily

00:20:58.323 --> 00:21:00.859
in say 1760, right?

00:21:00.859 --> 00:21:02.995
These were principles.

00:21:02.995 --> 00:21:08.000
And let's take any one of, of
the four self-evident truths,

00:21:09.101 --> 00:21:10.402 align:start size:88% position:13%
equality, rights,
consent, and revolution.

00:21:11.703 --> 00:21:13.205
These were principles

00:21:13.205 --> 00:21:16.742
that the revolutionaries
discovered and developed.

00:21:16.742 --> 00:21:21.747
So for me, the key year is 1765.

00:21:23.148 --> 00:21:26.184 align:start size:97% position:3%
And 1765 is the year in which
the Stamp Act was passed.

00:21:26.184 --> 00:21:31.189 align:start size:97% position:3%
And that's the moment when
everything in America changes.

00:21:31.923 --> 00:21:35.160
Now the second quotation

00:21:35.160 --> 00:21:37.462
that really drives
my interpretation

00:21:37.462 --> 00:21:38.730
of the revolution as a whole

00:21:38.730 --> 00:21:41.300
in addition to the one
by Thomas Jefferson,

00:21:41.300 --> 00:21:43.702 align:start size:97% position:3%
describing "The Declaration
of Independence",

00:21:43.702 --> 00:21:47.072
is one by John Adams.

00:21:47.072 --> 00:21:51.243 align:start size:94% position:6%
And John Adams late in
life in trying to describe

00:21:51.243 --> 00:21:54.146 align:start size:97% position:3%
to a friend what the causes
of the revolution were.

00:21:55.280 --> 00:21:58.216
He says that the revolution

00:21:58.216 --> 00:22:00.686
was not the war
for independence.

00:22:00.686 --> 00:22:03.255 align:start size:84% position:16%
The true American
revolution, he says,

00:22:03.255 --> 00:22:06.425 align:start size:84% position:16%
was put into effect
in the 15 years

00:22:06.425 --> 00:22:10.062 align:start size:97% position:3%
before a shot was ever fired
at Concord and Lexington.

00:22:10.062 --> 00:22:13.498
And the revolution, the true
American Revolution, he says,

00:22:13.498 --> 00:22:18.503
was a moral revolution in
the minds of the people.

00:22:19.905 --> 00:22:23.075 align:start position:0%
So that's what my book is really
about, the moral revolution

00:22:23.075 --> 00:22:25.344 align:start size:94% position:6%
that occurred in the minds
of the American people.

00:22:25.344 --> 00:22:30.215 align:start size:94% position:6%
And Adam's statement is
supported by Thomas Paine,

00:22:30.215 --> 00:22:35.020 align:start size:88% position:13%
who at that time also
in describing the causes

00:22:35.020 --> 00:22:36.888
and meaning of the
American Revolution

00:22:36.888 --> 00:22:40.158 align:start size:88% position:13%
said that because of
the American Revolution,

00:22:40.158 --> 00:22:44.529
the American people, he
said here with new ears,

00:22:44.529 --> 00:22:48.133 line:0%
see with new eyes and
think new thoughts.

00:22:48.133 --> 00:22:50.068 line:0%
And the new thoughts he said,

00:22:50.068 --> 00:22:53.438 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
had to do with the doctrine
of individual rights.

00:22:53.438 --> 00:22:57.209
It's the discovery of the
doctrine of natural rights

00:22:57.209 --> 00:23:01.012 align:start size:97% position:3%
by the Americans that really
is the most important,

00:23:01.012 --> 00:23:02.781
I think, revolutionary moment.

00:23:02.781 --> 00:23:05.784
And so what my book then does is

00:23:05.784 --> 00:23:08.153
it takes each one of the
four self-evident truths,

00:23:08.153 --> 00:23:10.389 align:start size:88% position:13%
equality, rights,
consent, and revolution,

00:23:10.389 --> 00:23:14.092 align:start size:94% position:6%
and it gives a kind of
intellectual history over,

00:23:14.092 --> 00:23:15.594
I devote two chapters

00:23:15.594 --> 00:23:18.130
to each of the four
self-evident truths.

00:23:18.130 --> 00:23:21.099
And I trace a kind of
intellectual history

00:23:21.099 --> 00:23:24.169
of, let's say the idea
of equality or rights

00:23:24.169 --> 00:23:25.604
from the late 17th century,

00:23:25.604 --> 00:23:28.874 align:start size:88% position:13%
from the English
philosopher, John Locke,

00:23:28.874 --> 00:23:30.842 align:start size:84% position:16%
up through the
early 18th century.

00:23:30.842 --> 00:23:33.512 align:start size:97% position:3%
But then really I spend
most of the time in the book

00:23:33.512 --> 00:23:37.816 align:start size:97% position:3%
discussing how the Americans
developed these ideas

00:23:37.816 --> 00:23:40.852 align:start size:84% position:16%
during the 1760s and
during the 1770s.

00:23:40.852 --> 00:23:44.022
And that's really how
the book is framed

00:23:44.022 --> 00:23:47.025
and that's really the
story that it's telling

00:23:47.025 --> 00:23:50.395 align:start size:97% position:3%
how the Americans
discovered these principles.

00:23:50.395 --> 00:23:55.400
- So we did equality, I want
to hit on the other three more.

00:23:56.635 --> 00:24:00.605
But first as they were
debating these things,

00:24:01.807 --> 00:24:03.341
one of the things that I
talk about all the time

00:24:03.341 --> 00:24:06.178
is they were saying, there
are truths outside of us.

00:24:06.178 --> 00:24:08.747 align:start size:97% position:3%
And then they're saying
these are God given rights.

00:24:08.747 --> 00:24:11.450 align:start size:97% position:3%
And then also we're
separating church and states.

00:24:11.450 --> 00:24:14.553
That concept is,
is pretty profound,

00:24:14.553 --> 00:24:16.254
at least in our modern times

00:24:16.254 --> 00:24:19.157 align:start size:84% position:16%
that men could do
something like that.

00:24:20.292 --> 00:24:22.060
Where they all sort
of on board that

00:24:22.060 --> 00:24:25.564 align:start size:84% position:16%
was there any pushback
that we can't,

00:24:25.564 --> 00:24:27.532
well, it's church and state,
certainly there was pushback,

00:24:27.532 --> 00:24:30.068 align:start position:0%
but that we can't say that
there are truths outside of us,

00:24:30.068 --> 00:24:33.205
it will lead to anarchy
or something like that.

00:24:33.205 --> 00:24:34.873
- Well, I think it's true to say

00:24:34.873 --> 00:24:37.909
that every single
American Revolution

00:24:37.909 --> 00:24:41.680
believed in the idea, as
I said earlier, of truth,

00:24:41.680 --> 00:24:45.851 align:start size:97% position:3%
of absolute objective, certain
permanent universal truths.

00:24:45.851 --> 00:24:50.021
But the question is what is
the source of those truths?

00:24:50.021 --> 00:24:53.425 align:start size:94% position:6%
- [Dave] Right.
- That's the key question.

00:24:53.425 --> 00:24:55.760 align:start size:81% position:19%
I can also tell
you that virtually

00:24:55.760 --> 00:24:57.829 align:start size:84% position:16%
every single American
Founding Father

00:24:59.197 --> 00:25:02.834 align:start size:97% position:3%
believed that for instance,
the concept of rights

00:25:02.834 --> 00:25:04.769
was grounded in nature.

00:25:05.937 --> 00:25:09.641
And by nature, I mean,
nature out there, right?

00:25:09.641 --> 00:25:12.844 align:start size:88% position:13%
You look out into the
world and you see nature

00:25:12.844 --> 00:25:15.080 align:start size:84% position:16%
and one of the really
important things

00:25:15.080 --> 00:25:17.315
that the Founding
Fathers did was

00:25:17.315 --> 00:25:21.253
they adapted the ideas of

00:25:22.354 --> 00:25:25.290 align:start size:84% position:16%
17th century,
English scientists,

00:25:25.290 --> 00:25:28.827
and philosophers like Francis
Bacon in his Novum Organum.

00:25:28.827 --> 00:25:32.030 align:start size:94% position:6%
And Sir Isaac Newton in
his Principia Mathematica,

00:25:32.030 --> 00:25:34.533
and John Locke, in his Essay
Concerning Human Understanding,

00:25:34.533 --> 00:25:39.037 align:start size:84% position:16%
And they took the
enlightenments ideas

00:25:39.037 --> 00:25:43.675
of the laws of nature, the
scientific laws of nature.

00:25:43.675 --> 00:25:47.546 align:start size:94% position:6%
And they attempted to
apply them to man, right?

00:25:47.546 --> 00:25:52.150
And they asked the question,
are there moral laws of nature?

00:25:53.351 --> 00:25:55.820
Which means laws that
are absolute permanent

00:25:55.820 --> 00:25:57.322
and universal, right?

00:25:57.322 --> 00:25:59.257 align:start size:97% position:3%
That you can look out into the
world and you can identify.

00:25:59.257 --> 00:26:03.562 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so, in order to
discover those moral laws

00:26:03.562 --> 00:26:07.999 align:start size:97% position:3%
and rights of nature, then you
have to study human nature.

00:26:07.999 --> 00:26:10.602 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so all of America's
Founding Fathers

00:26:10.602 --> 00:26:13.305
were students of
the enlightenment.

00:26:13.305 --> 00:26:17.042
They believed that
there were moral laws

00:26:17.042 --> 00:26:19.644 align:start size:88% position:13%
and rights of nature
that could be discovered

00:26:19.644 --> 00:26:22.013
by examining human nature.

00:26:24.182 --> 00:26:26.184
The more difficult question

00:26:26.184 --> 00:26:28.186
and where you do
see some division

00:26:28.186 --> 00:26:30.555
is, I think it's
true to say that

00:26:30.555 --> 00:26:33.625
most of the Founding Fathers
were practicing Christians.

00:26:33.625 --> 00:26:37.262
And so therefore they
believed that the laws

00:26:37.262 --> 00:26:41.066 align:start position:0%
and rights of nature we're
grounded in an ultimate source.

00:26:41.066 --> 00:26:44.069
And that ultimate source
of course, was the being

00:26:44.069 --> 00:26:46.605 align:start size:97% position:3%
as he's called in "The
Declaration of Independence".

00:26:46.605 --> 00:26:48.440
Now, but it's also the case

00:26:48.440 --> 00:26:51.443 align:start size:84% position:16%
that not all of the
Revolutionary Founders

00:26:51.443 --> 00:26:52.911
were practicing Christians.

00:26:54.012 --> 00:26:56.848
And so they discovered the laws

00:26:56.848 --> 00:26:59.217
and rights of nature in nature.

00:27:00.452 --> 00:27:02.420
And they didn't
go the next step.

00:27:02.420 --> 00:27:04.623 align:start size:84% position:16%
But the most important
thing to remember

00:27:04.623 --> 00:27:08.360 align:start size:94% position:6%
is that they all believed
in the moral laws

00:27:08.360 --> 00:27:12.130 align:start size:97% position:3%
and rights of nature, and
nature is the key term there.

00:27:12.130 --> 00:27:13.832
- So with all of this in mind,

00:27:13.832 --> 00:27:15.834 align:start size:84% position:16%
you're talking about
the enlightenment

00:27:15.834 --> 00:27:17.969 align:start size:94% position:6%
and how they were
incorporating these ideas

00:27:17.969 --> 00:27:20.605
and, had traveled,

00:27:20.605 --> 00:27:23.842 align:start size:94% position:6%
literally across the world
to form this new nation

00:27:23.842 --> 00:27:26.778 align:start size:97% position:3%
in a weird way, was this
sort of timing is everything

00:27:26.778 --> 00:27:28.647
that made America so great

00:27:28.647 --> 00:27:30.749 align:start size:97% position:3%
that it was sort of
following the enlightenment,

00:27:30.749 --> 00:27:33.785
finding a new land like
there was the opportunity

00:27:33.785 --> 00:27:38.423 align:start position:0%
and in many ways had the timing
been a little bit different

00:27:38.423 --> 00:27:40.692
and they wouldn't have been
able to find this new land

00:27:40.692 --> 00:27:42.160
right then?

00:27:42.160 --> 00:27:44.796 align:start size:97% position:3%
That these ideas really
never would have taken root

00:27:44.796 --> 00:27:45.697
the way they have.

00:27:46.865 --> 00:27:48.099 align:start size:84% position:16%
- No, I think that's
right, all right?

00:27:48.099 --> 00:27:51.936
So these ideas never
really quite took root

00:27:51.936 --> 00:27:56.074 align:start size:97% position:3%
at least socially and
politically in the old world

00:27:56.074 --> 00:27:58.643
where those ideas
came from, right?

00:27:58.643 --> 00:28:03.581
So there is something about
the American experience,

00:28:04.683 --> 00:28:06.818 align:start size:88% position:13%
the American experience
of leaving Europe

00:28:06.818 --> 00:28:11.823
and discarding all of the
old world manners and mores

00:28:12.991 --> 00:28:17.362
and institutions ideas
and starting de Novo

00:28:18.863 --> 00:28:22.167
starting in a new place.

00:28:22.167 --> 00:28:26.838
And they were able to
put into effect ideas

00:28:26.838 --> 00:28:29.574 align:start size:97% position:3%
that they never really
could have put into practice

00:28:29.574 --> 00:28:31.109
in the old world.

00:28:31.109 --> 00:28:34.079
Now, eventually, beginning
with the French Revolution

00:28:34.079 --> 00:28:36.514
and then over the course
of the following century,

00:28:36.514 --> 00:28:40.118 align:start size:97% position:3%
many of these ideas were
eventually put into practice

00:28:40.118 --> 00:28:41.653
in the old world,

00:28:41.653 --> 00:28:46.658 align:start size:97% position:3%
but you should view America
as a laboratory, right?

00:28:47.792 --> 00:28:52.797 align:start size:88% position:13%
A laboratory where
free men and women come

00:28:54.165 --> 00:28:58.436 align:start position:0%
and they are able to experiment
socially and politically,

00:28:58.436 --> 00:29:01.072 align:start size:84% position:16%
and they are able
to put into practice

00:29:01.072 --> 00:29:04.843
ideas that they simply they
would not have been able

00:29:04.843 --> 00:29:06.144
to put into practice in Europe.

00:29:06.144 --> 00:29:08.747 align:start size:81% position:19%
And so I think
it's a combination

00:29:08.747 --> 00:29:12.817 align:start size:84% position:16%
of these ideas coming
to the new world

00:29:12.817 --> 00:29:16.955
and then the fact that they
have a blank slate, right?

00:29:16.955 --> 00:29:21.960 align:start size:97% position:3%
They get to begin with a
Tabula rasa that is America,

00:29:23.061 --> 00:29:24.629 align:start size:84% position:16%
which was not
possible in Europe.

00:29:24.629 --> 00:29:28.166
And so it is a kind
of fortuitous meeting

00:29:28.166 --> 00:29:31.503 align:start size:84% position:16%
and was one of the
ways I think about it.

00:29:31.503 --> 00:29:35.740
When I think about
what America is,

00:29:35.740 --> 00:29:37.842
there was kind of a
fortuitous meeting

00:29:39.210 --> 00:29:43.648 align:start size:97% position:3%
of the ideas of "The
Declaration of Independence",

00:29:43.648 --> 00:29:46.518
which passed through
the Cumberland gap

00:29:47.719 --> 00:29:51.723
after the revolution and
where American pioneers

00:29:52.891 --> 00:29:56.161
like Davy Crockett
and Daniel Boone

00:29:57.462 --> 00:30:00.698 align:start size:84% position:16%
were able to
implement those ideas

00:30:00.698 --> 00:30:05.303
in a place that had
no institutions.

00:30:07.539 --> 00:30:11.476 align:start size:97% position:3%
So it was an extraordinarily
fortuitous meeting

00:30:11.476 --> 00:30:14.379
of both ideas

00:30:14.379 --> 00:30:18.983
and a place that had no
heritable institutions.

00:30:18.983 --> 00:30:21.152 align:start size:97% position:3%
- It's funny 'cause it
almost sounds like a miracle

00:30:21.152 --> 00:30:23.888
in a lot of ways, that all
of these things came together

00:30:23.888 --> 00:30:25.924 align:start size:94% position:6%
as these people are
traversing the new world,

00:30:25.924 --> 00:30:27.659 align:start size:81% position:19%
and they're having
this awakening

00:30:27.659 --> 00:30:29.194 align:start position:0%
and that's why at the
beginning of this conversation.

00:30:29.194 --> 00:30:32.330
I sort of likened it to what I
think is happening right now.

00:30:32.330 --> 00:30:36.067
I sense, although it feels
very authoritarian right now

00:30:36.067 --> 00:30:38.069
in draconian and kind of scary,

00:30:38.069 --> 00:30:41.706
especially, if you're in
California where I am.

00:30:41.706 --> 00:30:44.375
It's like there are
people waking up,

00:30:44.375 --> 00:30:46.711
I think people are
suddenly realizing

00:30:46.711 --> 00:30:49.881 align:start position:0%
what individual rights are,
what personal responsibility is.

00:30:49.881 --> 00:30:53.484
We may not see the fruits of
that in the next election,

00:30:53.484 --> 00:30:57.088
but, if this all goes
according to plan,

00:30:57.088 --> 00:30:59.424
I suppose you would say, well
we'll see the fruits of that

00:30:59.424 --> 00:31:00.558
in about 15 years.

00:31:01.960 --> 00:31:04.762 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, I think
that's probably right.

00:31:04.762 --> 00:31:06.831
I certainly hope it's right.

00:31:06.831 --> 00:31:08.366
I still believe,

00:31:08.366 --> 00:31:10.368
and I do write about
this a lot in the book.

00:31:10.368 --> 00:31:13.004 align:start size:84% position:16%
So one of the key
concepts in the book

00:31:13.004 --> 00:31:15.907
is this idea of the
spirit of liberty.

00:31:15.907 --> 00:31:19.110
And that's one of the
key moral concepts.

00:31:19.110 --> 00:31:21.880 align:start size:88% position:13%
I think of the
American Revolution was

00:31:21.880 --> 00:31:24.382 align:start size:88% position:13%
what I call the American
Spirit of Liberty.

00:31:24.382 --> 00:31:28.987 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I don't think any people,
in any place at any time,

00:31:28.987 --> 00:31:30.421
anywhere in the world

00:31:30.421 --> 00:31:32.957 align:start size:88% position:13%
has had what I call
this Spirit of Liberty.

00:31:32.957 --> 00:31:34.359
I mean, there's no other way

00:31:34.359 --> 00:31:39.163 align:start position:0%
to explain the American reaction
to the Stamp Act in 1765

00:31:40.632 --> 00:31:43.268
without understanding what
the Spirit of Liberty was.

00:31:43.268 --> 00:31:45.169 align:start size:88% position:13%
I mean, if you think
about what the Stamp Act

00:31:45.169 --> 00:31:47.705
or the Sugar Act or the
Townshend Acts were,

00:31:47.705 --> 00:31:50.441
I mean, they were having a
certainly compared to today,

00:31:50.441 --> 00:31:54.679
these were relatively small
taxes on the American people,

00:31:54.679 --> 00:31:56.648
but it wasn't just the taxes.

00:31:56.648 --> 00:31:59.317
And this is actually
directly comparable

00:31:59.317 --> 00:32:01.352
to the United States of today.

00:32:01.352 --> 00:32:04.989
So you can think about what the
British government was doing

00:32:04.989 --> 00:32:08.826
to the colonists in the
1760s, in the 1770s,

00:32:08.826 --> 00:32:12.096
as something akin to
the British deep state,

00:32:12.096 --> 00:32:14.632
the British deep state

00:32:14.632 --> 00:32:18.236 align:start size:94% position:6%
not only taxing and
regulating the colonists,

00:32:18.236 --> 00:32:22.173 align:start size:84% position:16%
but imposing
draconian regulations

00:32:22.173 --> 00:32:27.178 align:start size:94% position:6%
and forced by the military
on the American people.

00:32:28.680 --> 00:32:30.448
And so it's not just,

00:32:30.448 --> 00:32:32.083
it's not just the rallying cry,

00:32:32.083 --> 00:32:34.319 align:start size:84% position:16%
no taxation without
representation.

00:32:34.319 --> 00:32:39.324
No, the American people
really saw a much larger

00:32:40.458 --> 00:32:43.561 align:start size:84% position:16%
sort of web of laws
and regulations

00:32:45.964 --> 00:32:49.100 align:start position:0%
that were being imposed by this
kind of British deep state.

00:32:49.100 --> 00:32:50.768
And it ignited in them,

00:32:50.768 --> 00:32:54.739
I mean, the Americans
during this period,

00:32:54.739 --> 00:32:59.711
they were sort of overwhelmed

00:33:01.179 --> 00:33:03.081
by this kind of, and Edmund
Burke, the British statesman

00:33:03.081 --> 00:33:05.383 align:start size:84% position:16%
speaks of the American
Spirit of Liberty.

00:33:05.383 --> 00:33:07.518
He had never seen
anything like it.

00:33:07.518 --> 00:33:11.489
And so the Stamp Act
was like a trip wire

00:33:11.489 --> 00:33:13.691
that the American stepped over

00:33:13.691 --> 00:33:15.560
and it ignited the
Spirit of Liberty.

00:33:15.560 --> 00:33:18.696
And the Spirit of Liberty
is first and foremost,

00:33:19.897 --> 00:33:24.402
a recognition of power
and what power can do

00:33:24.402 --> 00:33:26.237
to ruin the lives of people.

00:33:26.237 --> 00:33:28.706 align:start size:97% position:3%
So the American
revolutionaries understood,

00:33:28.706 --> 00:33:30.908 align:start size:88% position:13%
I mean, there are
basic operating premise

00:33:30.908 --> 00:33:32.143
is that power corrupts

00:33:32.143 --> 00:33:35.013
and absolute power
corrupts absolutely.

00:33:35.013 --> 00:33:40.018 align:start size:97% position:3%
They understood that principle
to the core of their being.

00:33:40.752 --> 00:33:42.120
And the second they saw,

00:33:42.120 --> 00:33:46.124 align:start size:97% position:3%
even the slightest
intrusion of political power

00:33:46.124 --> 00:33:49.327
on their lives, either
in the form of taxes

00:33:49.327 --> 00:33:51.829
or regulations or
British troops,

00:33:53.297 --> 00:33:56.834
the trip wire was hit and
they reacted immediately.

00:33:56.834 --> 00:34:01.272
And I do not believe that the
American Spirit of Liberty

00:34:01.272 --> 00:34:03.741
in 2020 is dead.

00:34:03.741 --> 00:34:07.378
I think we are seeing the
American Spirit of Liberty

00:34:07.378 --> 00:34:11.182
being enacted by all
kinds of Americans

00:34:11.182 --> 00:34:14.452 align:start size:97% position:3%
all across the country,
particularly those Americans

00:34:14.452 --> 00:34:19.257 align:start size:97% position:3%
who have been forced by the
government to not work, right?

00:34:19.257 --> 00:34:22.260 line:0%
So in many ways you could
say that our situation

00:34:22.260 --> 00:34:26.164 line:0%
in May of 2020 is
considerably worse

00:34:26.164 --> 00:34:29.901 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
in terms of our rights and
liberties than it was in 1765.

00:34:29.901 --> 00:34:32.537 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I do think ordinary
everyday Americans

00:34:32.537 --> 00:34:35.940
are ready to stand
up for their freedom.

00:34:35.940 --> 00:34:38.376
- Yeah, well, I love that, I
love that you're seeing it too,

00:34:38.376 --> 00:34:40.278 align:start size:94% position:6%
and it's not just rattling
around in my brain,

00:34:40.278 --> 00:34:41.712
but I, I keep telling people,

00:34:41.712 --> 00:34:45.083 align:start size:97% position:3%
I'm seeing it in Los Angeles
when I walk around now

00:34:45.083 --> 00:34:47.218 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I'm walking, my dog
and neighbors come up to me

00:34:47.218 --> 00:34:48.619
and they know who I
am and they'll say,

00:34:48.619 --> 00:34:50.088 align:start size:97% position:3%
"You know, Dave, I'm starting
to think about things

00:34:50.088 --> 00:34:51.589
"a little differently."

00:34:51.589 --> 00:34:54.992
And you know, this is the
bastion of LA progressivism

00:34:54.992 --> 00:34:56.427
over here so I do
think there are

00:34:56.427 --> 00:34:57.662
some good things happening.

00:34:57.662 --> 00:34:59.130
So let's go back to
those four things.

00:34:59.130 --> 00:35:01.466 align:start size:97% position:3%
So we talked about equality,
we talked about rights,

00:35:01.466 --> 00:35:02.967
let's talk about consent.

00:35:04.669 --> 00:35:07.638
- Yeah, so it consent was also

00:35:07.638 --> 00:35:10.441
one of the early rallying
cries of the revolution.

00:35:10.441 --> 00:35:15.046
So the most famous
rallying cry in 1765,

00:35:15.046 --> 00:35:17.515 align:start size:84% position:16%
"No taxation without
representation."

00:35:17.515 --> 00:35:19.584
Representation is the idea

00:35:20.985 --> 00:35:25.957 align:start size:97% position:3%
that our government officials
have received the consent

00:35:27.325 --> 00:35:31.195 align:start size:97% position:3%
of ordinary everyday people
to make laws for them.

00:35:31.195 --> 00:35:33.131
But the purpose of these laws

00:35:33.131 --> 00:35:37.068 align:start size:84% position:16%
is to protect the
rights of individuals.

00:35:37.068 --> 00:35:41.272
So the third self-evident
truth of the declaration,

00:35:41.272 --> 00:35:44.208
that what I call the
so-called consent truth

00:35:44.208 --> 00:35:46.811 align:start size:84% position:16%
says that to secure
these rights,

00:35:46.811 --> 00:35:49.480 align:start size:94% position:6%
governments are instituted
among men, right?

00:35:49.480 --> 00:35:52.650 align:start size:84% position:16%
So it says that the
purpose of government,

00:35:52.650 --> 00:35:54.318
the sole purpose of government

00:35:54.318 --> 00:35:57.421 align:start size:84% position:16%
is to protect the
rights of individuals.

00:35:57.421 --> 00:35:59.957
It does not say that the
purpose of government,

00:35:59.957 --> 00:36:04.762 align:start size:84% position:16%
unlike certain
conservatives today,

00:36:04.762 --> 00:36:07.532 align:start size:88% position:13%
it does not say that the
purpose of government

00:36:07.532 --> 00:36:10.168
is to promote, let's say virtue.

00:36:11.035 --> 00:36:12.703
It says it's to protect rights.

00:36:12.703 --> 00:36:13.905
That is to say,

00:36:13.905 --> 00:36:17.608 align:start size:88% position:13%
to create spheres of
freedom for individuals.

00:36:17.608 --> 00:36:21.913
But the key though, is that
at the heart of the principle

00:36:21.913 --> 00:36:26.450 align:start size:84% position:16%
of consent is the idea
of self ownership.

00:36:26.450 --> 00:36:31.455
It says that each and every
man and woman owns himself,

00:36:31.455 --> 00:36:33.858
we are self sovereign.

00:36:33.858 --> 00:36:38.529
We are self-owning and
therefore self-governing.

00:36:38.529 --> 00:36:40.565 align:start size:84% position:16%
And because we
are, self-governing

00:36:42.600 --> 00:36:46.871 align:start size:97% position:3%
a legitimate government
only receives its legitimacy

00:36:46.871 --> 00:36:50.741
if it is based on the consent
of each and every individual.

00:36:50.741 --> 00:36:53.311
Now it doesn't mean
unanimous consent,

00:36:53.311 --> 00:36:55.513
but it, in the context
of the United States,

00:36:55.513 --> 00:36:57.315
it's majority consent,

00:36:57.315 --> 00:37:00.651
but we all consent to live
by the rule of the majority,

00:37:00.651 --> 00:37:02.787
which of course has its problems

00:37:02.787 --> 00:37:06.090
because majority's can
often be tyrannical.

00:37:06.090 --> 00:37:08.092
And this is precisely the
point that James Madison

00:37:08.092 --> 00:37:10.161
makes in the 10th
Federalist Essay.

00:37:10.161 --> 00:37:12.263
So there's always
a delicate balance

00:37:12.263 --> 00:37:17.201
between between consent and
the protection of rights

00:37:17.201 --> 00:37:19.804
but consent really is

00:37:21.138 --> 00:37:24.242 align:start size:94% position:6%
one of the core principles
of the revolution

00:37:24.242 --> 00:37:29.213
and it's one of the
core principles of
American civic life.

